.TH "vsearch-scramble" "1" "September 18, 2026" "version 2.32.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --scramble \[em] randomize the nucleotide order within each
fasta or fastq entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--scramble\f[R] \f[I]fastxfile\f[R]
(\f[B]--fastaout\f[R] | \f[B]--fastqout\f[R]) \f[I]filename\f[R]
[\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--scramble\f[R] reads the entries of a fasta or
fastq file (\f[I]fastxfile\f[R]) one by one and (pseudo-)randomizes the
order of the nucleotides within each sequence.
Output is written with \f[V]--fastaout\f[R], \f[V]--fastqout\f[R], or
both.
At least one output option is required.
If the input is in fasta format, \f[V]--fastqout\f[R] cannot be used, as
there are no base quality scores to carry over.
.PP
Scrambling is the within-entry counterpart of shuffling: the vsearch
command \f[V]--shuffle\f[R] randomizes the order of the
\f[I]entries\f[R] and leaves each sequence untouched, whereas
\f[V]--scramble\f[R] keeps the entries in their input order and
randomizes the nucleotides \f[I]inside\f[R] each sequence (see
\f[V]vsearch-shuffle(1)\f[R]).
To illustrate:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
>s1                      >s2                      >s1
AAACGT                   TGGGCA                   ACATAG
>s2   --- shuffle -->    >s1   --- scramble -->   >s2
TGGGCA                   AAACGT                   GAGCGT
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Scrambled datasets are useful as null models: they preserve the number
of entries, the entry order, the headers and abundance annotations, the
length of each sequence, and the exact nucleotide composition of each
sequence, while destroying all positional structure.
Each output sequence is a permutation of the bytes of the input
sequence, so uppercase and lowercase symbols, ambiguous nucleotide
symbols (see \f[V]vsearch-nucleotides(7)\f[R]), and their per-sequence
counts all pass through exactly.
Note that positional features are \f[I]not\f[R] preserved: runs of
lowercase-masked nucleotides are dispersed (only the masked fraction is
preserved), and the composition in words of two or more nucleotides
changes, unless preserved with \f[V]--scramble_kmer\f[R].
.PP
For fastq input, the quality string of each entry is always copied
through unchanged: the positional quality profile of each entry (and
therefore its expected error) is preserved exactly, and the pairing
between each nucleotide and its quality value is deliberately broken.
.PP
Sequences of length 0 or 1, and sequences consisting of a single
repeated symbol, pass through unchanged, as they admit only one
arrangement.
.PP
For reproducibility, the seed for the pseudo-random generator can be set
with the option \f[V]--randseed\f[R]; a given seed yields the same
result on any platform.
Each entry is scrambled with its own random sub-stream derived from the
seed and the entry ordinal, so for a given seed an entry\[cq]s scramble
does not depend on the other entries, nor on the presence or absence of
quality values.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.PP
At least one of the following output options is required:
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write scrambled sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write scrambled sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fastq format (see
\f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
Requires fastq input.
Quality strings are copied through unchanged (see the DESCRIPTION
section).
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--randseed\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Set the seed for the pseudo-random generator.
A given seed always produces the same results \[em] reproducibly across
platforms and independently of the number of threads \[em] which is
useful for replicability.
By default, vsearch uses a pseudo-random seed if \f[V]--randseed\f[R] is
not set, or set to the special value zero (0).
.TP
\f[V]--scramble_kmer\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Length \f[I]k\f[R] of the words whose counts are preserved by the
scrambling (range: 1 to 9; default is 1).
With the default value, each sequence becomes a uniformly random
permutation of its own nucleotides (mononucleotide scrambling).
With \f[I]k\f[R] = 2 or more, each sequence becomes a uniformly random
sequence with exactly the same counts of all \f[I]j\f[R]-mers for every
\f[I]j\f[R] <= \f[I]k\f[R] (dinucleotide scrambling, trinucleotide
scrambling, \&...), obtained by sampling a random Eulerian path of the
sequence\[cq]s de Bruijn graph, as in the classical Altschul-Erickson
dinucleotide shuffle and its \f[I]k\f[R]-mer generalization uShuffle.
Three consequences of \f[I]k\f[R] >= 2 to expect: the first and the last
\f[I]k\f[R] - 1 nucleotides always keep their positions; a sequence
containing at most one \f[I]k\f[R]-mer (length <= \f[I]k\f[R]) passes
through unchanged; and a short or low-complexity sequence may admit
exactly one valid arrangement, in which case the output equals the input
\[em] correct behaviour, not a failure to scramble.
Increasing \f[I]k\f[R] preserves more of the local structure and
therefore scrambles less.
For \f[I]k\f[R] >= 2, memory usage is proportional to the sequence
length (a few bytes per position, transiently).
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--fasta_width\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximal width of sequences when writing fasta files.
Longer sequences are folded and written on several lines.
Default width is 80 nucleotides.
Set to zero (0) to suppress folding.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] 33|64
Specify the \f[I]offset\f[R] used as the basis for the fastq quality
score when reading fastq files.
For example, an offset of 33 means that a quality value of 41 is
represented by the 74th ASCII symbol (33 + 41 = 74), which is `J'.
See \f[V]ascii(7)\f[R] for a view of the ASCII character set.
The offset value is either 33 or 64, default is 33.
.PP
The offset matters even to a command that never decodes a quality score:
it is what the reader compares the observed quality symbols against
before warning that the file may use the other encoding, it sets the
default of \f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] (the highest score the offset can
represent), and the sum rules on \f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] and
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] are stated in terms of it.
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--label_suffix\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the suffix \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers when writing fasta
or fastq files.
For example, with \f[V]--label_suffix \[dq];status=healthy\[dq]\f[R],
sequence header `>seq1' becomes `>seq1;status=healthy'.
.TP
\f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
Add a sequence length annotation (\f[V];length=integer\f[R]) to each
sequence header when writing fasta or fastq files.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
Messages are written \f[I]in addition\f[R] to the standard error, not
instead of it; use \f[V]--quiet\f[R] to silence the standard error copy
(see the \f[V]--quiet\f[R] entry for what it spares).
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R]
Retain whole sequence headers in output files.
With the vsearch command \f[V]--scramble\f[R], sequence headers are
never truncated, so this option has no visible effect.
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Replace sequence headers with the prefix \f[I]string\f[R] and a ticker
(1, 2, 3, etc.).
For example, with \f[V]--relabel \[dq]cluster:\[dq]\f[R], the first
sequence header becomes `>cluster:1', the second sequence header becomes
`>cluster:2', and so on.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R]
Retain old sequence identifiers by including them at the end of the new
headers, after a space.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the MD5 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The MD5 digest is a 128-bit value (16 bytes), represented using a string
of 32 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]md5(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_self\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the sequence itself.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the SHA1 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The SHA1 digest is a 160-bit value (20 bytes), represented using a
string of 40 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]sha1(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--sample\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the given sample identifier \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers
when writing fasta or fastq files.
For instance, if \f[I]string\f[R] is `ABC', the text
\f[V];sample=ABC\f[R] will be added to the headers.
\f[I]string\f[R] is silently truncated at the first `;' or whitespace
character (space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab or form
feed), so such characters should not be used in \f[I]string\f[R].
Other characters (alphabetical, numerical and punctuations) are
accepted.
When nothing is left after truncation \[em] an empty \f[I]string\f[R],
or one starting with `;' or a blank character \[em] vsearch issues a
warning and writes a bare \f[V];sample=\f[R] annotation.
.TP
\f[V]--sizein\f[R]
Use the abundance annotations present in sequence headers when reading
fasta or fastq file.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Entries without abundance annotations are silently assumed to be of
\f[V]size=1\f[R].
Has no effect with this command: abundance annotations are always parsed
(\f[V]--sizeout\f[R] therefore writes the true abundances whether or not
\f[V]--sizein\f[R] is given).
.TP
\f[V]--sizeout\f[R]
Add abundance annotations to sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Add the pattern \f[V];size=integer\f[R].
Existing \f[V];size=\f[R] annotations are reported unchanged; entries
without one receive \f[V];size=1\f[R].
For this command \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is not needed: abundance annotations
are always parsed from the input headers.
.TP
\f[V]--xee\f[R]
Strip expected error (ee) annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]ee=float[;]\f[R].
Expected error annotations are added by the synonymous options
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R] and \f[V]--eeout\f[R] described in
\f[V]vsearch-fastx_filter(1)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--xlength\f[R]
Strip sequence length annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]length=integer[;]\f[R].
Sequence length annotations are added by the \f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
option.
.TP
\f[V]--xsize\f[R]
Strip abundance annotations from sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Abundance annotations are added by the \f[V]--sizeout\f[R] option.
.SS ignored options
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Option is ignored: quality scores are not checked when reading fastq
sequences, so no score is rejected for being too high.
The argument itself is still validated, as the offset (see
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the maximal score may not exceed 126, the
last printable ASCII character.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Option is ignored: quality scores are not checked when reading fastq
sequences, so no score is rejected for being too low.
The argument itself is still validated, as the offset (see
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the minimal score must be at least 33, the
first printable ASCII character.
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Command is not multithreaded, option has no effect (a warning is printed
when more than one thread is requested).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Scramble a fasta file, with a fixed seed for reproducibility:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --scramble input.fasta \[rs]
    --randseed 1 \[rs]
    --fastaout scrambled.fasta
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Scramble a fastq file and write both fasta and fastq output (quality
strings are copied through unchanged):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --scramble input.fastq \[rs]
    --fastaout scrambled.fasta \[rs]
    --fastqout scrambled.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
